schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Fukuyama: social trust underwrites low-cost economic cooperation"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Political scientist Francis Fukuyama argued in "Trust" (1995) that social
    trust is the foundation of economic cooperation: high-trust groups
    coordinate complex activity at low cost, while low-trust groups need
    expensive enforcement and bureaucracy.
  evidence: >-
    Cited in the article from Fukuyama, "Trust: The Social Virtues and the
    Creation of Prosperity" (Free Press, 1995), listed in the article's
    References.
  interpretation: >-
    Used as the article's foundational argument for treating trust, not
    verification technology, as the real infrastructure of community
    finance — reframed as a blunt diagnostic question: "are you building
    social trust, or substituting technology for trust you have not earned
    yet?"
  uncertainty: >-
    Reproduces the article's own characterization of Fukuyama's 1995 thesis;
    not independently re-verified against the book's full argument for this
    ingestion.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/building-trust.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-06b003d4c0b2
  source_lineage: building-trust.md
